    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** serving as our premier play (77.51). Positioned at the top of the lineup against Peter Lambert, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a massive 14% dynamic platoon boost and the high-leverage scoring potential of the leadoff spot. Close behind, **Colt Keith** (69.64) presents an elite profile against the right-handed delivery of Young, bolstered by a combined 35% efficiency gain from park factors and pitcher skill set. While **Yordan Alvarez** (65.59) faces a tough assignment against Shota Imanaga, his xwOBA of .419 keeps him firmly in our starting rotation as an elite anchor, even with the projected platoon tax.\n\nManaging the roster\u2019s stolen base scarcity remains a priority, which makes the inclusion of **Caleb Durbin** (61.01) essential. Though batting 9th slightly suppresses his ceiling, the matchup against Bailey Ober is favorable, and the 87% steal-success rate against the pitcher's slow delivery makes **Caleb Durbin** a high-value asset despite his lower spot in the order. Our tightest decision involved **Carson Benge** (55.27), who sat just outside the cut-off. While **Carson Benge** earned a strong boost from a potential leadoff assignment, the optimizer ultimately leaned into the higher-floor efficiency of **Luke Raley** and **Kazuma Okamoto**, whose established roles provide more reliability in our daily game-cap management.\n\nCaution is warranted with several \"Lineup Pending\" designations, particularly regarding **Yordan Alvarez**, **Christian Yelich**, and **Junior Caminero**. **Christian Yelich** faces a difficult test against Yoshinobu Yamamoto; while his 52.05 score is passable, the risk of a high-strikeout performance is elevated by the pitcher\u2019s ability to control the basepaths, which limits **Christian Yelich**\u2019s ability to manufacture runs via the stolen base. We are monitoring **Trea Turner** (57.94) and **Kyle Schwarber** (56.48) closely